WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.390
Take the sentence: the student

2
00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.390
couldn't fit the laptop in the

3
00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.090
bag because it was full.

4
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.920
What does it refer to?

5
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.700
People answer instantly.

6
00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.590
Older language models often

7
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:13.570
guessed the laptop.

8
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.450
Today: scaled dot-product attention,

9
00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.050
why it is divided by the square root

10
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.990
of d, and how to teach the transformer

11
00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.510
without drowning students.

12
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.300
Attention lets every token build its

13
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.600
new representation as a weighted

14
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.500
average of all the tokens.

15
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.260
The weights say how relevant

16
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.640
each other token is.

17
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.420
So it can look straight at the bag.

18
00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.650
Each token makes three vectors.

19
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.340
Its query asks what it needs, each

20
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.820
key says what a token offers, and

21
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.340
values carry the content.

22
00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.090
Scores are queries times keys,

23
00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.270
divided by root d, then softmax.

24
00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.570
Here are one head's attention

25
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:01.790
weights from it to the other nouns.

26
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.150
Most of the weight goes to bag.

27
00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.910
The student and the laptop get little.

28
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.770
If query and key entries have mean

29
00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.110
zero and variance one, their dot

30
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.750
product has variance d.

31
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.910
Large scores push softmax into

32
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.270
saturation, where gradients vanish.

33
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.990
Dividing by root d keeps

34
00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.330
the variance near one.

35
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.820
Vaswani and colleagues' diagram has too

36
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.640
much in it for a first lecture.

37
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.790
Start with one head, three

38
00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.670
tokens and two dimensions.

39
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.440
Students compute the scores by hand, then

40
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.820
you add heads, layers and positions.

41
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:48.760
Quick check!

42
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.000
The key dimension d is sixty four.

43
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.260
By what number do we divide

44
00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.980
the raw attention scores?

45
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.850
Eight, the square root of sixty four.

46
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.840
Without it, scores would have a standard

47
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.660
deviation near eight and softmax would

48
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.540
almost always pick one token.

49
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.300
In the app, open the attention lab.

50
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.040
Tap the word “it” and see

51
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.300
which words it attends to.

52
00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.150
Switch the ending from “hungry” to

53
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.350
“fresh”, and watch its attention

54
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.570
move from “dog” to “milk”.

55
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.380
Your turn.

56
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.620
Attention alone treats

57
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.500
its inputs as a set.

58
00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.230
What does the transformer add

59
00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.650
so word order matters?

60
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.600
Positional encodings.

61
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.620
Remember: attention is a weighted

62
00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.900
average, scores are queries times keys

63
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.800
over root d, and position must be added.

64
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.140
Try it in your next lecture: give

65
00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.120
pairs two-dimensional query, key and

66
00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.880
value vectors for three tokens.

67
00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.820
They compute the scores, the softmax

68
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.940
and the output by hand, then compare

69
00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.660
with three lines of NumPy.
